<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heian period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/794">794</a>–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Heian jidai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Heian period was the last division of classical Japanese history, from 794 to 1185, beginning when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Chinese influence declined and a national culture matured: the syllabaries katakana and hiragana emerged, and court ladies wrote much of the vernacular literature. Real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a>, who intermarried with the imperial family, while the shōen estates grew more independent of imperial control. The warrior class entered court politics with the Hōgen Rebellion of 1156, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genpei-war">Genpei War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> seized power in 1185, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heian-period.jpg" alt="Heian period" width="330" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikiwikiyarou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:西寺復元模型.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 8th century, Buddhist temples were built at shrines and shrines at temples, and by the <b>Heian period</b> the theory of honji suijaku taught that the kami were local manifestations of the buddhas and bodhisattvas.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Japanese poetry compendium Kanwa taisho myoho renge-kyo, drawn from more than 120 collections of the <b>Heian period</b>, over 1360 poems refer to the Lotus Sūtra in their titles alone.</blockquote><a href="/work/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Heian period</b> monogatari meant prose fiction in the vernacular of the time, thought suitable only for women and children who could not read Chinese, the official language of government and religion.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The two were among the four clans that dominated Japanese politics in the <b>Heian period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
